---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const normalizedBase = baseUrl.endsWith('/') ? baseUrl : `${baseUrl}/`;
const withBase = (href: string) => href.startsWith('http') || href.startsWith('mailto:') || href.startsWith('#') ? href : `${normalizedBase}${href.replace(/^\//, '')}`;
const linkedinHref = 'https://www.linkedin.com/in/carlosjorques/';
const portraitUrl = withBase('/images/carlos-jorques-intro.webp');
---

<Layout title="About Carlos Jorques | Embedded Control Architect" description="About Carlos Jorques, an embedded control architect and technical leader for intelligent machines, embedded control software, automatic control, and production embedded systems.">
	<div class="about-page">
		<section class="about-hero" aria-labelledby="about-title">
			<p class="about-eyebrow">A closer look</p>
			<div class="about-hero__layout">
				<div class="about-hero__copy">
					<h1 id="about-title">About me</h1>
					<blockquote><p>What makes a machine truly intelligent?</p></blockquote>
					<div class="about-story">
						<p>I have been fascinated by that question for as long as I can remember. It led me to study Electrical Engineering and later pursue a doctorate in automatic control, drawn by the challenge of making machines sense, decide, and act reliably in the real world.</p>
						<p>For the past twelve years, I have worked on production embedded control software for electric trucks, autonomous machines, power electronics, and other complex vehicle systems. My work has taken me across Scania, Volvo Construction Equipment, and MAHLE Electronics, alongside international teams spanning software, electronics, control, mechanical engineering, testing, and calibration.</p>
						<p>The software is only part of the story. What defines my work more clearly is how I approach complex systems, how I help teams make difficult technical decisions, and how I turn uncertain problems into something people can understand and solve together.</p>
						<p>Where you go next depends on what brought you here.</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<img class="about-portrait" src={portraitUrl} alt="Carlos Jorques" width="768" height="1024" loading="eager" decoding="async" />
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section" aria-label="Visitor routes">
			<div class="section-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
			<div class="story-block route-list">
				<div><strong>Hiring or evaluating fit?</strong><p><a href={withBase('/cv')}>View CV</a> · <a href="#where-i-add-value">See where I add the most value</a> · <a href={withBase('/contact')}>Get in touch</a></p></div>
				<div><strong>Interested in how I work?</strong><p><a href="#approach">Read my approach</a> · <a href="#collaboration">Read about collaboration</a></p></div>
				<div><strong>Curious about how it began?</strong><p><a href="#origin">Read my story</a></p></div>
			</div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section" id="approach" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
			<div class="section-rail"><p>01 - Approach</p></div>
			<div class="story-block"><h2 id="approach-title">Understanding what should be built matters as much as building it</h2><p class="intro">I enjoy shaping technical direction early, identifying the decisions that matter, and making the reasoning behind them clear to the team.</p><p>I do some of my best work while a product or system is still taking shape, before its most important technical decisions have become difficult to change. Understanding what should exist, how the system should behave, and where its boundaries should be is just as satisfying to me as implementing it.</p><p>Experience has changed how I direct my curiosity. Earlier in my career, I wanted to explore every interesting problem. Today, I first ask which problem will make the greatest difference. Sometimes that means developing a new control strategy. Sometimes it means improving diagnostics, interfaces, or validation. Quite often, it means simplifying the architecture beneath everything else.</p><p>I also try to make tradeoffs visible. Technical decisions are stronger when the team understands the evidence, assumptions, constraints, and alternatives behind them. Clear reasoning helps decisions survive changing teams, new requirements, and the pressure of delivery.</p></div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section" id="collaboration" aria-labelledby="collaboration-title">
			<div class="section-rail"><p>02 - Collaboration</p></div>
			<div class="story-block"><h2 id="collaboration-title">Creating clarity so multidisciplinary teams can move forward together</h2><p class="intro">I help engineers from different disciplines build a shared understanding of the system before they try to solve it.</p><p>Complex systems rarely fail because one component is entirely wrong. Problems often appear at the boundaries between software and hardware, between teams, or between what the physical system can do and what the specification assumed it would do.</p><p>Before moving toward a solution, I like to make the behaviour of the whole system visible. When software, electronics, controls, mechanical engineering, testing, and calibration are working from the same picture, discussions become more concrete and much less focused on defending individual subsystems.</p><p>I see technical leadership as creating the conditions for good engineering. That includes clarifying ownership, resolving assumptions, connecting decisions to evidence, and helping the people closest to the system contribute effectively. My role is not to have every answer. It is to help the team ask the right questions and reach decisions that hold together.</p><blockquote><p>An elegant algorithm is only the beginning. The real engineering challenge is making it work reliably.</p></blockquote></div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section" id="origin" aria-labelledby="origin-title">
			<div class="section-rail"><p>03 - Origin</p></div>
			<div class="story-block"><h2 id="origin-title">From a wobbling robot to a career in control</h2><p class="intro">A small robot first drew me toward automatic control. Years of engineering taught me which problems are truly worth solving.</p><p>I started experimenting with electronics when I was eight, but the moment that set my direction came years later at Campus Party Valencia. I watched a line following robot I had built wobble and overshoot around the track while other robots moved smoothly through the same turns.</p><p>The hardware was capable of more. The difference was in the way the system was being controlled. That gap between what the physics allowed and what my logic produced pulled me toward automatic control.</p><p>Studying Electrical Engineering gave me the theoretical foundation to understand those systems properly. My industrial doctorate allowed me to investigate difficult control problems with scientific depth, question assumptions, and learn how to separate genuine improvement from apparent complexity.</p><p>Working in the automotive industry taught me the other half of the discipline. A sound idea must still survive limited computing resources, noisy signals, hardware variation, calibration, diagnostics, safety requirements, organisational constraints, and deadlines.</p><p>That combination of curiosity and production experience now shapes how I work. I enjoy exploring difficult problems, but I also know that engineering creates value only when a solution can be understood, maintained, validated, and delivered.</p></div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section" id="beyond-engineering" aria-labelledby="beyond-title">
			<div class="section-rail"><p>04 - Beyond engineering</p></div>
			<div class="story-block"><h2 id="beyond-title">Still building things, just with different constraints</h2><p class="intro">Curiosity continues outside work.</p><p>I spend much of my free time on 3D printing, lamp design, painting, electronics, and home automation. These projects give me room to experiment, learn unfamiliar skills, and build things simply because I find them interesting.</p><p>Basketball has also been part of my life since childhood. I still enjoy competing, helping younger players develop, and being part of a team working toward a shared goal.</p><p>Most importantly, time with my partner, our child, and our dog keeps work in perspective and reminds me to protect space for the people and experiences that matter beyond engineering.</p></div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section" id="where-i-add-value" aria-labelledby="value-title">
			<div class="section-rail"><p>05 - Where I add the most value</p></div>
			<div class="story-block"><h2 id="value-title">Where I add the most value</h2><p>I am most motivated by products whose behaviour is still being defined and where early technical decisions will have consequences for years.</p><p>That is where I contribute most effectively: combining hands on engineering with technical direction, helping teams understand the system, define its architecture, make difficult tradeoffs, and turn ambitious ideas into dependable products.</p><p>I am particularly drawn to electrified systems, autonomous machines, robotics, industrial automation, power electronics, energy systems, and other products where software interacts closely with physical behaviour.</p><p>I work best in focused, multidisciplinary teams where engineers can see the connection between their decisions and the real system. I value environments that combine technical ambition with practical responsibility, open discussion, and a willingness to test assumptions against evidence.</p></div>
		</section>

		<section class="editorial-section about-closing" aria-labelledby="closing-title">
			<div class="section-rail" aria-hidden="true"></div>
			<div class="story-block"><h2 id="closing-title">Let us build something that matters</h2><p>Are you developing a product whose behaviour depends on embedded control software? Are you looking for someone who enjoys shaping the system as much as implementing it?</p><div class="button-row"><a class="button primary" href={withBase('/contact')}>Get in touch</a><a class="button" href={withBase('/cv')}>View my CV</a><a class="button" href={withBase('/work')}>Explore selected projects</a><a class="button" href={linkedinHref} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Connect on LinkedIn</a></div></div>
		</section>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
	.about-page { width:min(calc(100% - 3rem),78rem); margin:0 auto; padding:0 0 5rem; }
	.about-eyebrow,.section-rail { font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:.82rem; font-weight:600; letter-spacing:.08em; line-height:1.4; text-transform:uppercase; color:var(--color-muted); }
	.about-hero { padding:6rem 0 4rem; }
	.about-eyebrow { margin:0; }
	.about-hero__layout { display:grid; grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1.35fr) minmax(15rem,20rem); gap:2.5rem; align-items:start; margin-top:1rem; }
	.about-hero h1 { max-width:none; font-size:clamp(3rem,4.8vw,5rem); font-weight:800; letter-spacing:-.05em; line-height:1.02; }
	.about-hero blockquote { margin:1.5rem 0 0; }
	.about-hero blockquote p { font-size:clamp(1.35rem,2vw,1.8rem); font-weight:600; letter-spacing:-.03em; line-height:1.35; }
	.about-story { display:grid; gap:1rem; margin-top:1.5rem; max-width:52rem; }
	.about-story p,.story-block p { margin:0; font-size:1.08rem; line-height:1.7; color:var(--color-text); }
	.about-portrait { width:min(100%,20rem); height:auto; border-radius:1.5rem; box-shadow:0 18px 40px rgba(23,23,23,.08); }
	.editorial-section { display:grid; grid-template-columns:16rem minmax(0,1fr); gap:2rem; padding:2.5rem 0; border-top:1px solid var(--color-border); scroll-margin-top:2rem; }
	.section-rail h2,.section-rail p { margin:0; max-width:none; color:inherit; font:inherit; letter-spacing:inherit; text-transform:inherit; }
	.story-block { display:grid; gap:1rem; max-width:52rem; }
	.story-block h2 { max-width:34ch; font-size:clamp(1.6rem,2.1vw,2.2rem); line-height:1.08; letter-spacing:-.05em; font-weight:800; }
	.story-block .intro { color:var(--color-muted); }
	.story-block blockquote { margin:.5rem 0 0; padding-left:1rem; border-left:2px solid var(--color-border-strong); }
	.story-block a { color:var(--color-link); text-underline-offset:.2em; }
	.route-list > div { padding-bottom:1rem; border-bottom:1px solid var(--color-border-soft); }
	.route-list strong { font-size:1.08rem; }
	.route-list p { margin-top:.35rem; color:var(--color-muted); }
	@media(max-width:1100px){.about-hero__layout{grid-template-columns:1fr}.about-portrait{width:min(100%,16rem)}}
	@media(max-width:720px){.about-page{width:min(calc(100% - 1.5rem),78rem)}.about-hero{padding:4rem 0 3rem}.editorial-section{grid-template-columns:1fr}.about-story p,.story-block p{font-size:1.05rem}.button{width:100%}}
</style>
